/* book: lead: Angle modulation carries the message in the phase of a sine carrier, not in its amplitude. The envelope stays fixed, so a limiter can strip amplitude noise before detection. Phase modulation (PM) moves the phase in step with the message. Frequency modulation (FM) moves the phase in step with the integral of the message, so the instantaneous frequency follows the message itself. FM radio, TV sound, */
/* book Figure 3.1.1: Worked example 1 drawn over two cycles of the 5 kHz tone. In FM the frequency swing copies the message, peaking at 6 kHz when m(t) is at +2 V; in PM it copies the slope of the message and peaks at 1.6 rad × 5 kHz = 8 kHz where the message crosses zero. */
