/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A sine carrier is s(t) = Ac cos θ(t).
   - Define the instantaneous frequency fi(t) = (1/2π) dθ/dt.
   - There are two ways to put the message in.
   - As a phasor: a vector of length Ac rotates at fc; modulation speeds it up and slows it down, but its tip stays on the circle of radius Ac, so the envelope stays Ac.
   - s(t) = Ac cos θ(t), fi(t) = (1/2π) dθ/dt
   - Ac is the peak carrier amplitude in V; θ(t) is the instantaneous phase in rad; fi(t) is the instantaneous frequency in Hz.
   - In PM the excess phase is proportional to the message: φ(t) = kp m(t), so s(t) = Ac cos(2πfc t + kp m(t)). kp is the phase sensitivity in radians per volt.
   - In FM the excess phase is proportional to the integral of the message: φ(t) = 2πkf ∫ m(τ) dτ, taken from 0 to t. kf is the frequency sensitivity in hertz per volt.
   - PM: s(t) = Ac cos(2πfc t + kp m(t)) FM: s(t) = Ac cos(2πfc t + 2πkf ∫m(τ)dτ)
   - kp is the phase sensitivity in rad/V; kf is the frequency sensitivity in Hz/V; m(t) is the message in V; the integral runs from 0 to t.
*/
