/* book exam traps: β is not Δf. Δf = kf Am depends on the message amplitude only; β = Δf/fm also depends on the message frequency. In FM, β falls as fm rises; in PM with a tone, kp Am is fixed and the equivalent Δf rises with fm. | The FM phase carries 2πkf ∫m dt. Without the 2π the phase is in cycles, not radians, so the deviation worked out from it is 2π times too small. Keep kf in Hz/V and keep the 2π. | PM and FM both have a constant envelope, but neither has a constant instantaneous frequency. An envelope detector cannot recover the message; a frequency or phase detector is needed. | Integrator and differentiator are easy to swap: integrator then phase modulator gives FM; differentiator then frequency modulator gives PM. | A tone-modulated wave written as Ac cos(2πfc t + β sin 2πfm t) could be FM or PM. Find the message only from the modulator type and its constant (worked example 2). */
