/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Take a tone-modulated FM wave s(t) = Ac cos(2πfc t + β sin 2πfm t).
   - s(t) ≈ Ac cos 2πfc t − βAc sin 2πfm t · sin 2πfc t
   - Ac is the carrier amplitude in V; fc the carrier and fm the tone frequency in Hz; β = Δf/fm.
   - Expanding the product gives a carrier plus one pair: Ac cos 2πfc t + (βAc/2)[cos 2π(fc + fm)t − cos 2π(fc − fm)t].
   - This is narrowband FM (NBFM).
   - For larger β the small-angle steps fail and the exact expansion is needed.
   - s(t) = Ac Σ Jn(β) cos 2π(fc + n fm)t
   - Jn(β) is the Bessel function of the first kind, order n; line n sits at fc + n fm with amplitude Ac |Jn(β)|; lines are spaced fm apart; J−n(β) = (−1)ⁿ Jn(β).
   - A negative Jn(β) is a 180° phase reversal, not negative power; power uses Jn².
   - Beyond about n = β + 1 the lines fall away fast.
*/
