/* book exam traps: Carson's rule is 2(β + 1)fm = 2(Δf + fm), not 2Δf and not 2βfm; those are only its large-β limit. | β = Δf/fm is dimensionless. Do not use Δf/fc: the carrier sets where the spectrum sits, not how wide it is. | NBFM and AM both show three lines, but the NBFM lower sideband is negative. An envelope detector gives almost no message from NBFM. | Sidebands are spaced fm apart, not 2fm. The pair at fc ± fm is one pair. | Jn(β) can be negative. That is a 180° phase reversal; the power of a line uses Jn², and the total stays Ac²/2. */
